<title>FRANCE: Parliament gives go ahead to equality directive.</title>
<headline>Parliament gives go ahead to equality directive.</headline>
<dateline>STRASBOURG, France 1996-11-12</dateline>
<text>
<p>The European Parliament accepted on Tuesday a proposed directive on equal pensions between women and men after a breakthrough under the consultation procedure.</p>
<p>Commissioner Marcelino Oreja, speaking on behalf of the European Commission, said he was prepared to accept key amendments demanded in the report by Portuguese Socialist Deputy Helena Torres Marques and the Parliament then gave the go-ahead.</p>
<p>The amendments relate to atypical and part-time work, a flexible retirement age and maternity leave.</p>
<p>The Commission was not however prepared to accept the fourth amendment relating to the calculation of pensions.</p>
<p>Oreja pointed out that an expert study on this complex subject had been commissioned and he said the Commission would discuss the findings with the womens' rights committee.</p>
</text>
